{"id":"q10","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"puzzle","question":"A doctor gives you 3 pills and tells you to take one every half hour, starting now. Will the pills last 90 minutes?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"The three pills are used up at the 60-minute mark, not 90.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"If each pill's effect lasts about 30 minutes, the medication's effects could cover 90 minutes.","confidence":0.3,"lean":1,"theta":0.4712,"phi":0.0},{"text":"\"Last\" could be read as 'keep you covered' rather than 'remain in your possession'.","confidence":0.25,"lean":1,"theta":0.3927,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Taking one pill every half hour starting now means doses at 0, 30, and 60 minutes.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Nothing is left to take at 90 minutes, so the supply is exhausted at 60 minutes.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The question naturally asks about the supply of pills, and the schedule is set by the doctor's instructions.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Reaching 90 minutes of dosing would require a fourth pill.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The pharmacological argument only matters if effects are assumed to outlast the dosing schedule, which the question does not state.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":-0.5,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":-0.3,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.95,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":-0.5,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":-0.3,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":-0.4,"relation"